riod  we also outlined some of the the longer  term  uh forecasts and the impacts of climate  change how will they be felt  so what we expect for the uk is in the  future for us to have more frequent  milder wetter winters  and that is basically due to as the  climate warms the atmosphere is able to  hold more moisture and that leads to  increases in rainfall and alongside that  we expect to see decreases in snow and  also more frequent in extremes  and indeed some of these changes are  already being seen in the observational  record  um so for example five  of the um wettest years  of the five of the ten wettest years on  record have happened since 2000 so  that's for records going back to the  1860s so this is not something that we'd  expect to happen by chance so there is  evidence that the climate  is already changing here in the uk  what do we need to do then with that  information in order to prepare for  what's to come  so some of the information that we're  able to provide now is really bringing  the climate change projections right  down from the global scale to the local  scale and that is helping us to prepare  and make sure that the uk is resilient  to climate change  some of the results that we recently  have is looking for example changes in  very heavy rainfall amounts that could  be important for flash flooding so  exceeding 30 millimeters in an hour for  example could be two times more frequent  by the 20 70s if we follow a high  emission scenario  and this is information is really  important for example in terms of how we  design our infrastructure in our cities  and you know urban drainage systems are  not designed based on these sort of  rainfall rates they're based on  historical rainfall rates that are  traditionally lower so this information  is really needed to go through in terms  of how we design our infrastructure our  bridges  so that we're resilient to these changes  in the future  okay professor lizzie kenden thanks very  much indeed for talking to us here this  evening  now in today's other climate news 7  trent has brought forward plans to clean  up its rivers by 16 years after a major  investigation into sewage treatment was  launched the water company said it will  work with farmers and other key sectors  of the economy to improve the quality of  rivers the environment agency and  regulators are investigating more than 2  000 sewage works over concerns that raw  sewage is being illegally pumped into  rivers  oil company shell has announced plans to  build a biofuel plant in singapore to  help reduce its overall emissions the  plant will make hydrogen from cooking  oil and animal fats which can then be  used to produce diesel for trucks jet  fuels and chemicals shell has committed  to halving its co2 emissions by 2030  and a french paralympian has completed a  75-mile journey across the largest  freshwater lake in south america to  raise awareness of environmental  pollution theo kieran and his two  teammates swam from one side of late  titicata to the other taking it in turns  to rest on a raft made out of recycled  materials theo said he hopes to  encourage people to stop leaving tons of  rubbish by the lake each year  young people should receive more  education about climate change according  to the youngest sitting mp nadia whittem  has introduced a motion in the house of  commons today calling for climate change  and sustainability to be integrated  throughout the primary and secondary  curriculum i spoke to her earlier and i  started by asking her what the bill is  all about  our climate education bill comes from  young people school students themselves  and it's calling on the government to do  three broad things to integrate climate  education across the national curriculum  so currently it's only taught in  optional subjects like triple science  and geography but it needs to be taught  in every subject so i mean for example  teaching about sustainable food  production and diets and food technology  teaching plumbing students how to  install low carbon heating systems  it's also calling for  that to be met with adequate teacher  training so that teachers are able to  deliver this climate education and the  third thing is for the decarbonisation  of the of the education sector  much quicker than is currently happening  so that children are learning in  retrofitted buildings for example  in practical terms nowadays how do you  see this working are you talking about  specific time allocated in the  curriculum or thread running through all  lessons and what ages are you aiming at  this needs to run like a thread  throughout the curriculum in the same  way that the climate crisis impacts  every part of our lives we've seen from  the pandemic pandemics will become more  frequent as a result of  climate breakdown  and young people need to be part of the  solutions to that so for a lot of people  2050 might seem like a like a long time  away for our generation will still be in  our working lives for children who just  started school in september they won't  even be 35 by 2050  so we're calling for this to happen from  nursery through to primary through to  secondary and fe education  you mentioned student involvement in the  bill how has that worked  this piece of legislation has come from  young people themselves they wrote it  they were in parliament today while i  presented it to parliament and we hope  that with the cross-party support that  we have from mps of all parties  including the conservative party  including three select committee chairs  from the environmental audit education  and bay select committees that the  government will listen to us and act and  see this as a continuation of the pledge  that it made during cop on climate  education which was good but doesn't go  far enough  now dear whittem thanks very much indeed  for talking to us here on the daily  climate show  thank you  a man known as french spider-man has  climbed up a 39-storey skyscraper in  frankfurt to raise awareness of climate  change alain robert got his nickname  from climbing some of the world's  tallest buildings using just his bare  hands after his climb alan said he was  disappointed with the outcome of cop 26  in glasgow and urged world leaders to do  more to end  deforestation now do stay with us  because coming up after the break i'm  going to be joined by dame julia king a  member of the committee on climate  change and the guardian columnist george  monbiot we're going to be discussing if  the uk is prepared for more flooding and  whether using bioenergy is really better  for the planet than fossil fuels we'll  see in a couple of minutes  hello and welcome back to the daily  climate show here on sky news and we're  getting straight to discussing the  climate issues of the day with dame  julia king chair of the adaptation  subcommittee of the committee on climate  change and the guardian columnist george  monbiot the met office has warned that  this winter could be wetter than normal  with households urged to be prepared for  the risk of flooding climate change  means that the uk can expect heavier and  more intense rainfall that change will  continue over the next few decades  whether or not we're on a path to net  zero so is the uk prepared for a wetter  future well the environment agency says  1.5 million households are in flood risk  areas but haven't got any preparations  julia  that's not a good figure to be starting  with how do you improve and increase  resilience and preparedness  gosh um  but of course the environment agency has  a very good flood and coastal erosion  risk management strap fully funds them  to deliver it but of course they can't  protect us from everything and we need  to to do some things for ourselves so we  need to make sure that everybody is  signed up for flood warnings uh if they  live in an area that has that is um at  risk of flooding and we need to make  sure that people understand the kind of  precautions they should be taking so for  example uh if there's a  significant risk  then they should have a bag packed they  should make sure they have their  medicines ready they should make sure um  they can either move  precious things upstairs if necessary  they should be prepared to leave the  house taking their pets with them those  sorts of things but but in general we  need to be designing um both our homes  and our cities to uh to take into  account that there will be much more  water around so we need to take on board  what the chinese are doing which is  they're designing sponge cities cities  that are designed to absorb water  because as we heard earlier we're going  to be getting more periods of very  intense rainfall which can lead to  surface water flooding in our cities and  that's probably one of the types of  flooding we are least well prepared for  in the uk at the moment  george  whose responsibility is it to ensure  that we're prepared is it government or  is it individual responsibility  well there's not much that we can do by  ourselves we do need government to  intervene and we need it to be far more  effective than it has been so far i'm  slightly puzzled by the focus the  particular focus that a lot of people  have on  how we um absorb water in cities um as  julia has just been discussing because  of course cities are just a very small  part of a river's catchment and they're  at the bottom of the catchment we also  need to be intensely focused on what's  happening in the rest of that catchment  how well is the land absorbing and  holding back the rain how well are the  rivers slowing down the flow and if as  we've so often done we've canalized and  dredged the rivers we tend to speed up  the flow towards the cities until it  hits the nearest urban pinch point and  then bursts its bank what we need to do  is have rivers which hold back the water  for as long as possible so that they  allow the the flow to be attenuated to  be slowed down to come down instead of  in one great big pulse in a long slow  flow and that means allowing them once  more to meander to braid to form islands  and other obstructions that just slow  that water down similarly on the  hillsides let's get some trees back  let's get some um terracing in in in  important places let's do everything we  can to slow down the flow throughout the  entire catchment rather than sitting  there like rabbits in the headlight  waiting for this great wall of water to  come down and hit us and then wondering  what to do about it yeah sitting ducks i  guess julia are we getting the focus  wrong then when we when we prioritize  cities in our on our planning around  cities george arguing that we need to  take our focus further upstream  and no we need uh we need all of those  things  i was particularly talking about surface  water flooding which is the response to  periods of short short relatively short  periods of very intense rainfall so it's  not water in cities it's not water  coming from the rivers it's actually the  water coming from the sky and and  surface water flooding is something we  are less well prepared for in the uk  than we are for for coastal flooding and  river flooding but george is right of  course we should be using nature-based  solutions um to try and slow down the  the rate at which water comes into our  rivers when we have wet weather so those  sorts of things are very important as  well  george are there other areas i know  particularly you've talked about canada  british columbia where we should be  looking to  it's really shocking what's happening  there i mean it's a classic case of a  compound climate disaster so so as you  know there was this massive heat dome  over that region of western canada and  the western us um in the summer and  there were huge fires  those not only destroyed a lot of trees  they also helped to bake the soil all  that ensures that water runs off it more  and now we see this massive dump of  rainfall you know probably also  exacerbated by climate breakdown  and a lot of the communities in british  columbia are now flooded and in some  cases that flood water has frozen and is  likely to remain frozen for the whole  winter so what they're going to do  i mean it's just it's almost  unimaginable the situation that some  communities are in it's really really  horrible and i'm surprised there's so  little coverage of it in the news at the  moment  okay let's move on to our second topic  and this is bioenergy now bioenergy  refers to power that is generated from  organic matter known as biomass we heard  earlier in the program that shell is  planning a biofuel plant in singapore in  part to lower its emissions  and if we look at electricity being  generated in britain right now  we can see uh seven percent of that is  coming from biomass most of which is  from burning wood chips it is officially  designated as renewable because those  wood chips come from sustainable  plantations so how big a role should  biofuels play in the transition to a low  carbon economy uh george this is your  topic let's put that question to you  first i think the role should be about  zero it's an outrageous scam and um so  if you look at a situation like drax  they appear to have lower carbon  emissions because of a carbon accounting  convention which says that you don't  count carbon dioxide produced from  burning mashed up trees because one day  trees will grow back to replace the ones  you've mashed up well the one day is the  crucial issue it could take decades to  absorb that carbon which you've just  released and we don't have advocates we  need to front load our environmental  action they're doing exactly the  opposite and what we're seeing is a  massive new wave of forest destruction  caused by this supposedly green  technique of burning biomass and we're  seeing in some cases old growth forests  in europe and in north america being  failed to be turned into wood chip and  burnt is an absolute disgrace and it  needs to stop  julie biomass is classified as a  renewable fuel  is it clean or is it dirty as  george has described  um biomass can make a useful  contribution i think we know we have a  lot of commercial forestry in the uk  that forestry needs to be thinned there  are trees um which are not fit for um  producing uh you know for using in in  construction and things  those thinnings can be used usefully for  biomass and indeed if we can use  appropriately produced biomass and i  agree with george it's really critical  that we ensure it is appropriately  produced if we can use that in the  future with carbon capture and storage  that can play  a small but significant role in in  giving us negative emissions and getting  helping us get to net zero but i agree  with him it does have to be extremely  carefully managed uh george is it's  better than fossil fuels isn't it  no it isn't um forestry thinnings and  waste are always used as a trojan horse  to enable much bigger operations to go  ahead we're sold the idea on the basis  that it'll be waste products used it's  invariably we end up with  fresh material being used entire forest  areas being cut down and fed into these  burners it's really disgraceful and the  whole idea of bioenergy with carbon  capture and storage it is unicorn poo  it's a total pipe dream and honestly we  should just drop this nonsense and get  on with a genuinely clean energy  revolution based on renewables and i  think also fourth generation nuclear  ten seconds to get a final answer from  you then julia  um  well appropriately used sustainable  biomass forms part of the climate change  committee's pathway to net zero  thank you very much very succinct to  educate george monbiot thanks very much  indeed for joining us here on the daily  climate show this evening  that is everything from us for today  i'll be back tomorrow and on the show  i'll be joined by dr cc who from the  university of oxford's environmental  change institute and also richard black  senior associate at the energy and  climate intelligence unit  but do stay tuned because coming up next  on sky news tonight  new advice from the government in  northern ireland amid growing concern  over the rise in covid cases thanks very  much for watching us and we will see you  again here tomorrow on the daily climate  show  [Music]  you

